--TEST--
A class that carries neither `#[Json\Derive]` nor a written `fromJson` can be read out of no document, and the call that asks for one is refused while compiling — `rule:core-classes/derive-attribute`, `rule:core-classes/derive-generates-what-is-missing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/derive-attribute`: participation is opt-in, and the two
// doors into it are this attribute and the hand-written half the derive would
// otherwise generate. The accepted side of the bound, first door.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
final class Row {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor(int $n) {
        $this->n = $n;
    }
}

// The accepted side, second door. `rule:core-classes/derive-generates-what-is-missing`
// lets a class write a half itself, and `fromJson` is the half this call needs,
// so the class has opted in as squarely as the attribute does — it records no
// field list precisely because there is nothing left to generate.
final class Written {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor(int $n) {
        $this->n = $n;
    }

    public static function fromJson(mixed $value): Written {
        return new Written(0);
    }
}

// The refused side, one declaration different: neither door. The class stays
// well formed for everything else it does — the program can still build one —
// so the refusal is of the **call** that asks for one out of a document.
final class Plain {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor(int $n) {
        $this->n = $n;
    }
}

final class Legacy {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor(int $n) {
        $this->n = $n;
    }
}

var $row = Core\Json::decodeAs<Row>("{\"n\":1}");
var $written = Core\Json::decodeAs<Written>("{\"n\":1}");
echo $row->n, $written->n, "\n";

// The report quotes back the class it was handed, so the sentence is not a
// constant: two classes, two of them, differing in the one place the caller
// chose.
var $plain = Core\Json::decodeAs<Plain>("{\"n\":1}");
var $legacy = Core\Json::decodeAs<Legacy>("{\"n\":1}");
echo $plain->n, $legacy->n, "\n";

// The class is answered before the document is, and a compile-time refusal is
// as far forward as that goes: this text is not JSON at all and the class is
// still what the report names.
var $malformed = Core\Json::decodeAs<Plain>("{oops}");
echo $malformed->n, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0821]: `Plain` carries no `#[Json\Derive]` and declares no `fromJson`, so `Core\Json::decodeAs` has no codec to read one with
%A
error[E0821]: `Legacy` carries no `#[Json\Derive]` and declares no `fromJson`, so `Core\Json::decodeAs` has no codec to read one with
%A
error[E0821]: `Plain` carries no `#[Json\Derive]` and declares no `fromJson`, so `Core\Json::decodeAs` has no codec to read one with
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
